1. `checking a vector array empty or not` : d.empty() will return true otherwise false . 


2. `taking element from back of a vector array` : int a = d.back(); d is a vector of integer 
    elements . 
    
    
3. `removing last element from a vector array` : d.pop_back();


4. `erase any element of a vector array as per index` : d.erase(d.begin()+i); will erase 
    the element at ith position .
    
    
    
5. as we know that xoring with 0 will return that number and xoring that number with that number
   will return 0 so by this we can confirm whether a particular element is occuring for odd number
   of times or not.
   
   
6. `cout << ranges::fold_left(d,0,plus{}) << '\n';` : `std::ranges::fold_left` is a C++23 standard 
    library algorithm (found in the `<algorithm>` header) that "reduces" or "folds" a collection of
    items into a single result by processing them strictly from left to right. It is the modern, 
    safer equivalent of the older `std::accumulate` function.
	  1. `Ex:` ranges::fold_left(d, 0, plus{}) --> d = {5,10,15} then first time 0+5,then 5+10 , 
	  then 15 + 15 return 30 .
	  
	  
7. `ranges` : this is modern approach for finding maximum and minimum .
	  1. `How to use` : int a = ranges::max(d) d is a vector array of integers and this ranges is the
	  modern way of finding maximum element as it will pass d.begin() and d.end() automatically
	  and also dereference the iterator also. and int a = ranges::min(d) for minimum . 
	  
	  
8. `Wrapping number in single quote` : as you wrapped any number in single quotes then we 
    convert that number in ascii code as '1' == 1 false because '1'  will be converted as per 
    ascii number . Wherease You cannot compare a string pointer directly to an integer like 
    `"1" == 1` (it will cause a compilation error or compare a memory address to `1`). 
